<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bonn</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bonn is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>. Founded as a Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> fortress, it became the residence of the archbishop-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> of Cologne from the late 13th century, and Beethoven was born there in 1770. From 1949 to 1990 it was the seat of government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a>, and it kept several federal ministries after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> became the capital.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bonn.jpg" alt="Bonn" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Matthias Zepper · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:General_view_over_bonn_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bonn lies on both banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a>, about 24 kilometres south-south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, in the southernmost part of the Rhine-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhr">Ruhr</a> region. It covers 141.2 square kilometres, and almost three quarters of the city lie on the left bank. In 1969 the independent towns of Bad Godesberg and Beuel and several villages were incorporated into Bonn, producing a city more than twice as large as before. Earlier, on 1 June 1904, Poppelsdorf, Endenich, Kessenich and Dottendorf had been incorporated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>People lived in the area long before the city. Evidence includes a 14,000-year-old double burial at Oberkassel and a trench and wooden palisades on the Venusberg dating to about 4080 BCE. The Roman governor Agrippa had settled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ubii">Ubii</a> there in 38 BCE, and in 12 BCE the first fortified Roman camp on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> was built. In 1989 the city marked its 2,000th anniversary, counting from that camp. Bonn was later part of the Roman province <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania-inferior">Germania Inferior</a>.</p><p>From 1597 to 1794 it was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/electorate-of-cologne">Electorate of Cologne</a> and the residence of the Archbishops and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">Prince-electors</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>. The Altes Rathaus, in Rococo style, was built in 1737 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clemens-august-of-bavaria">Clemens August of Bavaria</a>, and the Kurfürstliches Schloss, the prince-elector's residence, is now the main building of the University of Bonn.</p><p>Bonn served as the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a> from 1949 until 1990 and as the seat of government of reunified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> until 1999, when the government moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>. Historians often call the period of West Germany's capital there the Bonn Republic. The Basic Law, Germany's constitution, was made in the city. A political compromise after reunification, the Berlin-Bonn Act, kept a significant federal presence in Bonn.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Bonn is a federal city, with the title Bundesstadt, and a population exceeding 320,000. It holds the primary seats of six federal ministries and twenty federal authorities, and the global headquarters of Deutsche Post DHL and Deutsche Telekom. Twenty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> institutions have their seats there, among them the secretariats of the UN Framework Convention on Climate Change and the Convention to Combat Desertification. Beethoven's birthplace stands in Bonngasse near the market place, and he was baptised at St Remigius.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bonn” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The new state chose <b>Bonn</b> as a provisional capital to emphasise that the two-state solution was temporary.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-germany">Federal Republic of Germany · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 1970s Ursula Rößler-Köhler began a working group at the University of <b>Bonn</b> that became the Book of the Dead Project, which keeps documentation and photographs of 80% of the surviving copies and fragments and publishes monograph and manuscript series.</blockquote><a href="/work/book-of-the-dead">Book of the Dead · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>By 338 votes to 320, the parliament of reunified Germany chose Berlin over <b>Bonn</b> as the seat of government.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-20">20 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Charles the Simple, king of West Francia, and Henry the Fowler, king of East Francia, signed a &quot;pact of friendship&quot; aboard a ship in the middle of the Rhine near <b>Bonn</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-7">7 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After French armies occupied the left bank of the Rhine, local supporters of the Revolution in towns such as <b>Bonn</b>, Koblenz, Cologne and Aachen proclaimed a Cisrhenian Republic in September 1797, with the encouragement of General Lazare Hoche, who favoured a separate state allied to France.</blockquote><a href="/state/cisrhenian-republic">Cisrhenian Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>After long conflicts with the citizens and the archbishop's defeat at the Battle of Worringen in 1288, Cologne became in effect a free city, and the electors moved to <b>Bonn</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/electorate-of-cologne">Electorate of Cologne</a></li></ul></section>
